model {
  extend basalt.toolsGateway.appHostNavigation {
    metadata {
      constraint "The shared scoped-session check retains the accepted-read principal and absolute expiry beside its non-authorizing navigation reference, and client disposal closes the committed-change feed while cancelling navigation. `apps/client` `navigate` sends a declarative destination, either a tab of the page's own app from a closed list or an account ID, with the page's own version, and resolves with the host's answer or false after a bounded wait. The client runtime owns the entire wait and its cleanup; disposal cancels pending sends and settles them false before a later queued continuation can post; a page that is not embedded resolves false at once. Only the active app screen answers, and only messages whose window is the app's own embedded document at its origin; other windows, including frames the page opens, get no answer. Before posting, the client confirms its own scoped sign-in through a fresh open of the host's version watch, which authorizes the scoped session and its parent login, and reads only the status. The entire operation, including preflight, shares one three-second deadline. Request IDs are unique across client instances, and each host authorization read and its completion belong to their own request; one request cannot consume the atom's result for another. Normal completion and interruption both remove client callbacks; disposing a posted request sends cancellation before completing it false; cancellation and receiver expiry discard pending host work, so a delayed authorization cannot navigate after the caller stops waiting. The serving host injects only a non-authorizing reference to the existing Better Auth verification record keyed by the scoped-token digest. The receiver answers only after a fresh, uncached dashboard read verifies that exact unexpired scoped record against the caller's parent login, user, app, organization, current origin, access and active version; an ended login, lost access, an unknown destination, a malformed account ID or any version other than the one read is refused before navigation. No raw token crosses the frame boundary, and no record, index, migration or separate authorization system is added. Accepted requests push a host route; one frame owner inside the signed-in boundary, above the dashboard and the app's own window, keeps each document running across both, so Back returns to the same document. Organization pages read it through their host, which closes other organizations' documents; without that owner a host keeps its own documents as before. The owner's layer has no box and its documents stay fixed over their slots in the main region. Navigation grants no access beyond the destination's own permissions. The legacy usage-history back message, host protocol 41 and the framework version are unchanged."
    }
  }
}
